From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan <ryan@esterlines.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 14:21:09 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] LibURL+LibWeb+LibWebView: Let the embedder serve URL schemes
 of its own

An embedder can now register a URL scheme that it serves from the UI
process, as a browser serves its extension pages, with
WebView::register_url_scheme() and a handler that answers each request
with a file and response headers.

URLs of such a scheme get tuple origins of (scheme, host, port), as
http(s) URLs do, so that each host is an origin with storage of its own.
They are potentially trustworthy, like a packaged application's, so
their documents are secure contexts. They are fetch schemes, so
navigations, subresources, fetch() and workers fetch them, and CORS
requests to them are allowed, with the handler deciding the CORS
headers.

WebContent asks the UI process for each such URL, as it does for a local
file, since its sandbox keeps it from reading anything itself. The
handler is told whether the asking process hosts a document of the URL's
own origin, which site isolation makes the difference between a page's
own requests and those of pages that embed it.

Renderers learn the registered schemes from --embedder-scheme arguments
when they launch.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
